# Line endings are decided here, not by each machine's git config.
#
# The working tree may be CRLF (this is a Windows machine; core.autocrlf=true).
# The repository is always LF. Without this file, .editorconfig's `end_of_line = lf`
# is advice to editors and nothing more, and a tool that reads bytes — a hash check,
# a regex over a line ending — behaves differently per clone.
#
# sed/awk/grep in Git Bash strip \r on read, so they cannot be trusted to report this.
# Count bytes instead:  tr -cd '\r' < file | wc -c
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF even on Windows, or bash fails with '\r': command not found
*.sh text eol=lf

# Never touched
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mov binary
*.webm binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary
*.m4a binary
*.sqlite binary
